{
 "result": "fibmod.py is a fast-doubling Fibonacci mod 1e9+7. It reduces n modulo P = 2(M+1), the Pisano period bound, then walks the bits of n from the top, computing F(2k) and F(2k+1) per bit with one modulo each. It reads all stdin tokens, computes each value, and writes the results joined by newlines. It matches the sample output (in-0/out-0 PASS), and assay accepted it at baseline median 4.7937 with 1.4% noise, up from 2.58 before the Pisano reduction (+83%). I'm stopping here because the remaining ideas are small micro-optimizations that would have to beat the 3% win threshold, and the last variant I tried (removing a modulo per bit) lost.",
